{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS002738","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 4 candidates are direct matches to the target concept (alcohol dependence / alcohol use disorder): PGS002738 and PGS002739 have trait_efo="alcohol dependence" with phenotyping_reported="Alcohol use disorder"; PGS000201/PGS000202 are labeled "Problematic alcohol use" but their phenotyping_reported is still "Alcohol use disorder (DSM-5 criteria count, log-transformed)", which is endpoint-faithful.

Best-supported choice: PGS002738. It most directly matches the target disease concept in trait fields (trait_reported="Alcohol use disorder", trait_efo="alcohol dependence") and provides interpretable PRS discrimination evidence via an odds ratio for extreme risk stratification (OR top 10% vs rest = 1.96, 95% CI 1.54–2.51) in a European evaluation (n=7,900). It also comes from a disease-focused AUD context ("Evaluating risk for alcohol use disorder: Polygenic risk scores and family history.") with multi-cohort development listed (MVP, UKB).

Main limitation keeping this from HIGH_QUALITY: across candidates, comparable PRS-only metrics (AUC, R2) are largely missing, and PGS002738 has "Unknown" covariates, limiting comparability/optimism assessment versus PGS000201 (ΔR²=0.01192) and PGS000202 (ΔR²=0.00456), which report incremental variance explained but in different settings and with smaller/variable validation sizes. With these heterogeneous and incomplete metrics, the preference for PGS002738 is evidence-based but not definitive."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005254","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Among the 8 returned candidates, only three are direct concept matches to the target disease aortic stenosis by trait_reported/trait_efo (PGS005252, PGS005254) or by being an aortic-valve-function quantitative trait with aortic stenosis EFO listed (PGS005255/PGS005256). The hypertrophic cardiomyopathy scores (PGS000739/PGS000778/PGS004910/PGS004911) are not direct matches.

Between the two explicit aortic stenosis models, PGS005254 is preferred because its endpoint is aligned (phenotyping_reported: incident aortic stenosis) and its reported evaluation avoids heavy clinical comorbidity covariates (age/age^2/sex/PCs only), making any reported association more comparable to a PRS-focused signal. It also reports a time-to-event effect size (HR=1.64, 95% CI 1.50–1.78) with a sizable validation sample (n=244,450). In contrast, PGS005252 reports a high C-index (0.87) but uses multiple cardiovascular comorbidity covariates (e.g., type 2 diabetes, hypertension, CAD, hyperlipidemia, BMI, smoking, renal failure), which domain guidance flags as likely to inflate full-model discrimination and reduce comparability to PRS performance.

Limitation: neither aortic stenosis candidate reports PRS-only AUROC/R² or incremental metrics in the provided metadata, so selection relies mainly on endpoint alignment and covariate comparability plus effect-size evidence; this keeps the direct-match recommendation below high-confidence."}

