  "rationale": "All listed candidates are concept-level direct matches to hypothyroidism, but several are endpoint-mismatched for generic hypothyroidism risk (e.g., therapy-induced hypothyroidism in cancer patients: PGS000759/PGS000761/PGS000820) or use potentially non-comparable covariates. The strongest apparent performer (PGS005218; AUROC=0.859) is down-ranked because its covariates include near-outcome thyroid biomarkers (TSH, T4, anti-TPO), which the provided domain_knowledge flags as likely inflating full-model AUROC and not demonstrating PRS quality. Among remaining generic hypothyroidism endpoints, PGS004935 reports a clear discrimination metric (full_model AUROC=0.70) with a reasonably sized European validation (n=8,417) and an OR per SD of 1.5, while many other generic-endpoint models either lack comparable AUC/R2 (e.g., PGS002766 reports OR only; PGS001816/PGS002024 report partial-r only) or come from framework/portability papers with weaker per-trait optimization priors. Confidence is only Moderate because PGS004935 has unknown covariates and the evidence set does not provide PRS-only metrics for most competitors, leaving residual ambiguity about true PRS contribution across candidates."
}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000054","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 5 candidates are direct matches at the concept level (trait_efo is late-onset Alzheimer’s disease for all). Endpoint fidelity is strongest for PGS000053/PGS000054/PGS000334/PGS004918, which all report a late-onset Alzheimer’s disease diagnosis endpoint (PGS000053/PGS000054 specifically familial late-onset AD, which the provided domain policy treats as a direct match for late-onset neurodegenerative disease deployment). PGS002289 has weaker endpoint fidelity because its phenotyping_reported is a cognitive test interaction phenotype rather than AD diagnosis, despite the trait label.

Among the endpoint-faithful set, comparable PRS metrics (PGS-only AUC/R²) are largely missing; thus effect-size evidence becomes important. PGS000054 reports the strongest per-SD effect size (OR=1.73, 95% CI 1.57–1.93) versus PGS000053 (OR=1.29, 1.21–1.37). PGS000334 reports an R²=0.191 but it is listed under other_metrics and covariates are unknown; per the domain guidance and the local heritability sanity-check (h2_obs≈0.0084; h2_liability≈0.0269), this very large R² is difficult to interpret as PRS-comparable and may reflect non-comparable reporting or inflation, so it cannot be used as strong ranking evidence. PGS004918 reports only a full-model AUROC=0.731 with unknown covariates and a very small validation sample (n=136), and the guidance cautions against treating full-model AUROC as PRS-only evidence.

PGS000054 is therefore preferred on the visible evidence because it combines a direct diagnosis-aligned endpoint family with the strongest reported PRS-linked effect size, while alternatives either have weaker effect-size support (PGS000053), potentially non-comparable performance reporting (PGS000334), non-PRS-comparable full-model AUROC with tiny validation (PGS004918), or endpoint mismatch (PGS002289). The main limitation is that AUC/R² metrics are absent for PGS000054 and its evaluation ancestry is AMR (Hispanic/Latin American) rather than EUR, making cross-candidate comparability and general deployment support uncertain; hence the outcome is sub-optimal rather than high-quality."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000054","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 5 candidates are direct matches at the concept level (trait_reported/trait_efo align to late-onset Alzheimer’s disease). Endpoint fidelity is strongest for PGS000053/PGS000054/PGS000334/PGS004918, which explicitly use late-onset Alzheimer’s disease (with PGS000053/PGS000054 labeled as familial late-onset LOAD—still considered a direct match per provided disease-family policy). PGS002289 is down-ranked because phenotyping_reported reflects a cognition-by-age interaction outcome rather than AD diagnosis, despite the LO-AD label.

Among the endpoint-faithful options, only PGS000053 and PGS000054 provide a clear PRS-linked effect size (OR per SD), and PGS000054 shows the strongest effect estimate (OR=1.73, 95% CI 1.57–1.93) with a substantial validation sample (n=3,324). PGS000334 reports an R²=0.191 from a C+T model with unknown covariates; per the domain guidance, such a high R² for a simple thresholding method plus unclear covariates is a comparability red flag, so it is not strong enough to outrank the more interpretable per-SD OR evidence. PGS004918 reports only a full-model AUROC (0.731) with tiny training/validation (n=439/136) and unknown covariates; domain guidance cautions against treating full-model AUROC as PRS-comparable, so this metric is not decisive.

Overall evidence remains limited (no PRS-only AUROC/R² for the leading models; covariates often unknown; ancestry suitability depends on intended deployment), so the best choice is supported but not definitive, yielding a DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL outcome."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS000054","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"All 5 candidates are direct matches at the trait/EFO level (late-onset Alzheimer’s disease). Endpoint fidelity is strongest for PGS000053/PGS000054/PGS000334/PGS004918, which report late-onset Alzheimer’s disease (with PGS000053/PGS000054 explicitly framed as familial LOAD, which domain guidance treats as still a direct match for late-onset neurodegenerative disease). PGS002289 has a clear endpoint mismatch in phenotyping_reported (cognition-by-age interaction rather than AD case status), so it is down-ranked despite large validation size.

Among the remaining endpoint-faithful options, performance reporting is sparse/incomparable: PGS000053 and PGS000054 provide per-SD ORs (no AUC/R²), PGS000334 reports an R²=0.191 (very high for a simple C+T score and flagged by the provided domain guidance as potentially non-comparable/inflated, with unknown covariates), and PGS004918 reports only a full-model AUROC=0.731 in a very small validation set (n=136) with unknown covariates, which domain guidance cautions against treating as PRS-comparable. Given these limitations, effect-size evidence plus endpoint alignment becomes the main differentiator, and PGS000054 shows the strongest reported per-SD association (OR=1.73, 95% CI 1.57–1.93) with the same disease-focused publication context as PGS000053.

Main limitation: no candidate provides clearly PRS-only AUROC/R² with transparent covariates in a large, clean external validation; several metrics are missing or potentially non-comparable, so the recommendation is not high-confidence."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS005220","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait “sleep apnea”, including multiple generic sleep apnea scores (PGS003204–PGS003218) and obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) scores (PGS003479, PGS003857, PGS003858, PGS005219, PGS005220). Domain knowledge explicitly states OSA is the clinically dominant form of sleep apnea, so OSA-labeled models are acceptable and may be preferred when better supported.

Among the OSA models, PGS005220 has the strongest visible performance signal (full-model AUROC=0.79; OR=1.53 [1.39–1.68]) with a reasonably large validation sample (n=21,975) and multi-cohort development context (FinnGen, MGBB, MVP). Its covariates include only basic demographics/PCs plus BMI; while BMI adjustment can affect interpretability/comparability, it is explicitly part of this score’s design (“UnadjustedBMI” label) and is less concerning than heavy clinical biomarker panels.

However, confidence is limited because (i) reported AUROC values for the ExPRSweb sleep-apnea scores are near chance (~0.49–0.53) but are full-model metrics and not clearly PRS-only, making cross-model comparability weak; (ii) several OSA models exist with similar high AUROC (e.g., PGS005219 also AUROC=0.79 but higher OR=1.98), and the context does not provide enough endpoint/metric detail to decisively separate adjusted vs unadjusted BMI variants for a generic “sleep apnea” target; and (iii) some phenotype fields appear inconsistent for certain candidates (e.g., PGS003479 phenotyping_reported=DBP), reducing overall certainty."}

{"outcome":"DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL","best_model_id":"PGS003381","confidence":"Moderate","rationale":"Direct-match candidates are present for the target trait uterine carcinoma, including (i) an exact-label uterine cancer PRS (PGS001795; phenotyping_reported: “Uterine cancer”) and (ii) multiple endometrial cancer PRSs (PGS000075, PGS000786, PGS002735–PGS002737, PGS003381, PGS004244). Per the provided domain policy, endometrial carcinoma is the clinically dominant subtype of uterine carcinoma and should be treated as a strong direct match; therefore the best-supported choice should come from the endometrial/uterine-endometrial carcinoma set rather than relying solely on the exact-label framework score.

Among the endometrial-aligned models, PGS003381 has strong endpoint fidelity (“uterine endometrial carcinoma”), uses only basic covariates (age, top 20 PCs) avoiding heavier clinical augmentation, and shows the most favorable visible discrimination evidence in this context (full_model_auc=0.761 with R²=0.11 in European evaluation; n=144,479). Competing endometrial candidates either have weaker reported discrimination (e.g., PGS002735–PGS002737 full_model_auc 0.53–0.56; PGS004244 provides only HR without AUROC/R²) or similar AUC but more epidemiologic covariates including family history and BMI (PGS000075/PGS000786), which reduces cross-model comparability.

Limitation: the key reported AUROC for PGS003381 appears to be a full-model metric rather than an explicitly PRS-only AUROC, and the exact-label uterine cancer model (PGS001795) is also a plausible direct match but comes from a global biobank/framework context with modest PRS-only R² and only moderate AUROC (0.66). This mixture of endpoint scope (uterine vs endometrial) and non-uniform metric reporting prevents a high-confidence, unambiguous winner, so the overall direct-match selection is sub-optimal rather than clearly high-quality."}
